Method references.

Very often, a lambda does nothing but call one existing method — x, arrow, print x; or s, arrow, Integer parse-int of s. For that case, Java has an even shorter, clearer form: the method reference, written with a double colon. It names the method you'd call, and lets the compiler wire the arguments through.

Here's the shorthand. List dot for-each, x, arrow, print x — that lambda becomes list dot for-each, System-dot-out, double-colon, println. Identical behaviour. Names dot stream dot map, s, arrow, s to-upper-case, becomes map of String, double-colon, to-upper-case. So System-dot-out double-colon println reads as "the println method of System-dot-out". When a lambda's whole body is a single method call, whose arguments line up with the lambda's parameters, the method reference says the same thing with less noise — and it often reads more like intent, "uppercase each", than like mechanics.

There are four kinds, and they differ in what the target of the call is. First, a static method — Type, double-colon, method name. Integer double-colon parse-int is the same as s, arrow, Integer parse-int of s. Second, a bound instance — a specific object you already have, then double-colon, method. System-dot-out double-colon println is x, arrow, print x. Third, an unbound instance — a Type, double-colon, an instance method, where the instance becomes the first parameter. String double-colon to-upper-case is s, arrow, s to-upper-case; the receiver is supplied per call. And fourth, a constructor — Type, double-colon, new. Array-list double-colon new is empty-parens, arrow, new array-list. Point double-colon new is x, y, arrow, new point of x and y.

The one that trips people up is the third kind. String double-colon to-upper-case looks static, but it isn't — the object that the method runs on becomes the lambda's first argument. So a function from string to string, assigned String double-colon to-upper-case, takes a string and calls to-upper-case on it.

Here's a quick way to read them by shape. If the double-colon is on an instance — System-dot-out double-colon, or my-list double-colon add — the receiver is fixed; it's that object. But if it's on a type — String double-colon, or Integer double-colon — then either it's static, or the receiver is the first argument passed in. And constructor references, double-colon new, build a new object from the arguments, which is perfect wherever a supplier or a factory is wanted. A supplier of a list, assigned array-list double-colon new, is empty-parens, arrow, new array-list. And stream dot generate, of Random double-colon new, uses the constructor as a supplier.

So when do you prefer which? Reach for a method reference when the lambda is purely a call to a named method — it's shorter, and it states the operation by name. Keep a lambda when the body does anything more — extra arguments, a transformation, multiple statements, or when a reference would actually be less clear. So dot map of String double-colon trim is clean, as a reference. But map of s, arrow, s dot trim dot to-lower-case stays a lambda, because it's two calls, which a reference can't express. And filter of s, arrow, s-length greater than three stays a lambda, because that's logic, not a single call. Method references and lambdas are interchangeable wherever both fit; choose whichever reads more like what you mean. With the syntax now covered, both long and short, the next sections name the interfaces these lambdas satisfy — starting with function.
